<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Three Gorges</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Three Gorges are the narrow, steep-sided canyons of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> between Fengjie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> and Yichang in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>: the Qutang, Wu and Xiling gorges. For centuries they were the main route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> and the middle Yangtze, and controlled access to the lands of the ancient Ba people. The Three Gorges Dam, completed in the 2000s, flooded much of the gorges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/three-gorges.jpg" alt="Three Gorges" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tan Wei Liang Byorn · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qutang_Gorge_on_Changjiang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
